learning: The SW lead over plain S2 at n=100 and n=200 is a tail effect, not a skewness effect: at exact 5% size with 20000 paired draws across three seeds, Shapiro-Wilk leads S2 beyond two paired SE under every symmetric alternative tested (Student t df 3, 5, 10, logistic, uniform) at both n, and on t df 20 at n=200, while both tests hold 5% under N(0,1). Under skewed gamma SW led only for sqrt(beta1) above 0.9, so S2 wins where skewness dominates and SW wins where tails dominate.
